        <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Suzhou: Fans & Silk Accessories</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Suzhou''s Pingjiang Road and Shantang Street are lined with shops selling hand-painted silk fans (团扇), embroidered pouches, and hair ornaments. The Suzhou Silk Museum offers context before you shop, and the prices are more reasonable than Shanghai or Beijing.</p>
        <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hangzhou: Silk Scarves & Umbrellas</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Hangzhou Silk City is a multi-story market with hundreds of vendors. Oil-paper umbrellas (油纸伞) from nearby Yuhang are a specialty — lightweight, hand-painted, and surprisingly durable.</p>
        <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Beijing: Jade & Jewelry</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Beijing''s Hongqiao Pearl Market and Panjiayuan Antique Market are treasure troves for jade pendants, embroidered shoes, and vintage accessories. Bargain hard — starting at 30-40% of the asking price is standard.</p>
        <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Xi’an: Hanfu Hair Accessories & Ornaments</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Xi’an’s Hanfu rental streets near the South Gate are lined with accessory shops selling traditional hairpins, floral hairpieces, and tassels. The Muslim Quarter area has stores specializing in bronze mirrors and Tang dynasty-style ornaments. Budget 30–80 RMB for a quality hairpin set or 50–150 RMB for a complete hair accessory ensemble.</p>
        <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">Guizhou: Miao Silver & Embroidery Textiles</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">For truly handcrafted pieces, head to the Miao villages near Kaili in Guizhou. Miao silversmiths create elaborate headdresses, neck rings, and bracelets using techniques passed down for generations. Textile markets in Kaili sell hand-embroidered bags, belts, and fabric pieces direct from artisans. These make exceptional souvenirs with a story behind them. Expect to pay 100–500 RMB for quality silver pieces and 50–200 RMB for embroidered textiles, depending on complexity.</p>
        <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-10 mb-4">What to Look For When Buying</h2>
        <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">When buying traditional accessories, check for authentic regional materials: Suzhou silk, Beijing jade, Guizhou silver, Hangzhou brocade. Mass-produced souvenirs flood popular tourist streets, so walk a block or two from the main drag for better quality at lower prices. For fans and umbrellas, verify the frame is bamboo (not plastic) and the painting is hand-applied rather than printed. A good hand-painted silk fan costs 80–200 RMB; a machine-printed one, 20–40 RMB. Most shops will wrap purchases carefully for travel, but bring a small padded pouch for delicate jade or silver items.
    </div>
